await fs.mkdir('course/drafts/t03-013', { recursive: true });
await write('overview', [head + ' — Big-picture orientation and mental model', '', '# Problem and purpose', '', 'Performance work begins when a user-visible operation is slow, inconsistent, or costly. The task is to locate where time and resources go, establish which workload is affected, and test a change without shifting cost elsewhere. “The database is slow” is a symptom description, not a diagnosis.', '', '# Plain-language mental model', '', 'Imagine a service desk. Requests arrive, receive service, and sometimes wait for a worker, memory, storage, or another transaction. A query plan is the route through the work; runtime counters describe what happened; wait evidence describes where a request paused. No single counter tells the whole story. First identify the operation and interval, then join evidence from the application, active requests, history, and server resources.', '', '# How the parts fit together', '', '1. Define the affected operation, user impact, time range, and recent changes.', '2. Establish whether the symptom is latency, throughput, errors, or resource cost, and segment it by endpoint/query and parameter shape.', '3. Compare a representative baseline with current behavior. Account for traffic, engine restart, counter reset, and cache state.', '4. Find evidence at the right level: live requests and waits for “now”; Query Store for persisted query/plan history; Extended Events for a targeted event timeline; operating-system counters for shared CPU, memory, and I/O context.', '5. Form a testable bottleneck hypothesis, change one factor in a controlled environment or canary, and compare both user outcomes and resource cost.', '', '# Concept map', '', '| Question | Evidence | Limitation |', '| --- | --- | --- |', '| What is waiting now? | Active requests and waiting tasks | A snapshot can miss a transient wait |', '| What changed for this query? | Query Store plans and time-bucketed runtime statistics | Aggregation can hide parameter and tail behavior |', '| Is the instance saturated? | OS counters, scheduler, memory, file I/O, workload concurrency | Server-wide totals can hide a single bad query |', '| What happened at a particular event? | A scoped Extended Events session | Collection has overhead and retention limits |', '| Did a change help? | Equivalent before/after workload and a control/canary | Unlike workload windows are not causal evidence |', '', '# End-to-end walkthrough', '', 'Suppose an endpoint’s p95 latency rises after a deployment while its request rate is unchanged. Record the time window and build/setting changes. Confirm the endpoint’s database calls and parameter cohorts. Compare its Query Store intervals and plans to the prior window; inspect live blocked requests if the problem is ongoing. If the query plan’s logical reads rose, check the actual workload and index/statistics hypothesis; if requests wait on log flushes, compare log-file latency and commit frequency. Apply one reversible change to a realistic replay or canary. Compare p50/p95/p99, successful throughput, errors, logical reads, and CPU per operation; revert if the predefined guardrail worsens.', '', '# When to use it and tradeoffs', '', 'DMVs are excellent for a current snapshot and counters but many are volatile or cumulative. Query Store supplies historical context but consumes database storage and may stop capturing when read-only. Extended Events can capture precise evidence but must be narrowly scoped. Query hints or forced plans can mitigate an urgent regression but need monitoring and an unforce/rollback path. A change that lowers average duration while reducing throughput or worsening p99 is not automatically a win.', '', '# What later lessons cover', '', 'The concepts lesson develops evidence scope and measurement. The three reference lessons cover diagnostic APIs/tools, bottleneck interpretation, and controlled remediation. Practice and interview lessons apply them to production-style scenarios. Execution-plan operator interpretation belongs to t03-009; index internals to t03-008; transaction semantics to t03-010.', '', '# Recent version changes', '', 'SQL Server 2025 adds expression cardinality-estimation feedback, OPPO at compatibility level 170, and `OPTIMIZED_SP_EXECUTESQL`; their eligibility and configuration differ. Query Store for readable secondary replicas is available in SQL Server 2025 but is opt-in. Do not infer that a feature is active merely because the engine version supports it. [IQP features](' + urls.iq + ') [secondary Query Store](' + urls.qsSecondary + ') [SQL Server 2025 changes](' + urls.news + ')', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

await write('concepts', [head + ' — Core concepts and how they fit together', '', '# Learning objectives', '', '- Turn an imprecise performance complaint into a bounded, testable question.', '- Distinguish a current wait from accumulated wait history and query-attributed evidence.', '- Build a timeline from application latency, Query Store, DMVs, and targeted events.', '- Test a remedy with representative traffic and a reversible deployment.', '', '# Prerequisites', '', 'Understand query plans, indexes/statistics, transactions, connection pools, and basic latency/throughput measures. The operator-by-operator plan guide is in t03-009; this lesson concerns diagnosis across a workload.', '', '# Core concepts', '', '## Start from workload and user impact', '', 'Name the operation, affected users, start time, current status, and recent changes. Separate response time from throughput and error rate. Segment by query identity, parameter distribution, tenant or request type where safe, and deployment version. If an endpoint spends time waiting for a pooled connection before SQL begins, a database query plan cannot explain that portion of latency.', '', '## Evidence has scope and a lifetime', '', 'A live request DMV is a snapshot of what is executing now. `sys.dm_os_waiting_tasks` shows present waits; `sys.dm_os_wait_stats` accumulates completed waits since engine startup/reset and has no query attribution. Query Store stores query/plan records and interval-aggregated runtime data, while plan-cache statistics depend on cached plans. File and index counters also have reset windows. Every comparison should record collection time, instance/database, uptime or reset, and the query window.', '', 'For SQL Server 2022 and later, many server performance DMVs require `VIEW SERVER PERFORMANCE STATE` rather than the older `VIEW SERVER STATE`; consult the exact DMV permissions and grant a narrowly scoped diagnostic identity. [DMV permissions](' + urls.dmv + ') [wait counter lifetime](' + urls.waits + ')', '', '## Waits are leads, not diagnoses', '', 'A wait means work could not proceed immediately. `PAGEIOLATCH` is associated with reading pages into the buffer pool; more reads can create more such waits even if the storage itself has not changed. `WRITELOG` points to log flush waiting, which could arise from log latency or commit pattern. `SOS_SCHEDULER_YIELD` is one CPU-pressure clue, not a proof; correlate runnable work and CPU. `CXPACKET` and `CXCONSUMER` are parallel-exchange coordination waits and need a plan/skew/concurrency interpretation. `PAGELATCH` concerns an in-memory latch and differs from a page-I/O latch. Do not sort a wait list and blindly apply a fix.', '', '## Query Store and plan cache answer different questions', '', 'Query Store persists query text, plans, and aggregated runtime measures per time interval. It can show plan changes and regression windows after a cached plan disappears. Its data is aggregated: means do not disclose every parameter cohort or p99. Check capture state, storage usage, interval and retention. Plan forcing requests reuse of a recorded plan but optimizer forcing can fail; retain a validation and unforce step. Query Store is on by default for new SQL Server databases starting in 2022, not necessarily existing databases. Query Store for readable secondaries is available in SQL Server 2025 but not on by default. [collection](' + urls.qsCollect + ') [management](' + urls.qsManage + ') [forcing](' + urls.qsTune + ') [secondary availability](' + urls.qsSecondary + ')', '', '## Resource symptoms need corroboration', '', '- **CPU:** compare CPU time per operation, concurrent runnable demand, and throughput. A CPU-heavy query can be locally expensive without saturating the server.', '- **Reads and I/O:** logical reads represent buffer pages touched; physical reads require storage. Compare deltas by file and query, not only server wait totals.', '- **Memory grants:** sorts/hashes can queue on `RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE` or spill to tempdb when grants and estimates do not fit. Inspect request grants, plan estimates, concurrency, and memory feedback.', '- **Locks:** identify the blocking chain and transaction age. A deadlock is a cycle chosen for victim rollback; ordinary blocking can persist without a cycle.', '- **Tempdb:** determine if the evidence is allocation latch contention, metadata contention, I/O, or query spill before selecting a fix.', '- **Workers and pools:** SQL worker starvation (`THREADPOOL`) and application pool exhaustion are different queues at different boundaries.', '', '## Use an experiment, not folklore', '', 'A sound experiment states the hypothesis, predicted measure, affected population, control window, rollback trigger, and owner. Change one factor; compare under equivalent arrival rate, parameters, data volume, cache conditions, and background activity. Include distribution metrics (p50, p95, p99), successful throughput and errors, and normalized resource use such as CPU/logical reads per operation. A local query win can increase write cost or harm another endpoint.', '', '# Mechanisms and relationships', '', 'A practical evidence chain is: user timer -> application pool/driver -> SQL session/request -> query/plan -> waits and resource counters -> storage/OS. Collect each layer only as needed, use a monotonic time basis where possible, and correlate IDs carefully. Extended Events should collect only the events and fields that test the hypothesis; event files are more durable than bounded in-memory buffers but require disk, access control, and retention.', '', 'Missing-index suggestions are candidate signals. They do not price write amplification, overlap, storage, filtered designs, or whole-workload impact. An index change should be justified with representative query evidence and regression checks. Statistics changes can trigger recompilation; a hint or forced plan can age poorly as data changes. Each intervention needs an expiry/review point.', '', '# Worked examples', '', 'A query becomes slow only for a large date range. Query Store shows two plans but interval averages mix small and large parameter values. Capture a representative parameter distribution, compare plan/runtime behavior per cohort, and inspect live execution evidence. Do not conclude the “new plan is bad” from one aggregate row. Test a candidate mitigation against both cohorts and total throughput; preserve the ability to revert.', '', '# Misconceptions and edge cases', '', '- Large cumulative wait time is not necessarily the current bottleneck; it can predate the incident or reflect a busy workload.', '- A wait type does not name its root cause, and server-wide wait stats do not identify the responsible query.', '- `sys.dm_exec_query_stats` is plan-cache data, not an audit-grade historical record.', '- A Query Store average can hide high p99, a rare spill, or a parameter subgroup.', '- Lower logical reads can still be a regression if CPU, writes, concurrency, or tail latency gets worse.', '- Killing a long transaction can incur lengthy rollback and does not remove the underlying cause.', '- A client timeout does not prove SQL Server stopped executing; check cancellation, retries, and connection occupancy.', '- Missing-index output is not a command to create every suggested index.', '', '# Knowledge checks with answers', '', '1. Do `sys.dm_os_wait_stats` rows show waits currently in progress? **No.** They record completed waits cumulatively since start/reset; use waiting tasks or active requests for a current snapshot.', '2. Does `PAGEIOLATCH` prove the storage hardware is faulty? **No.** Excess physical reads, storage latency, and workload changes must be separated.', '3. What does Query Store add over plan cache? **Persisted query/plan and interval history**, subject to capture and storage configuration.', '4. If an app times out while SQL shows no expensive query, what boundary should be checked? **Pool acquisition, driver/network time, request cancellation, and whether work continued.**', '5. Why keep p99 and throughput when evaluating an index? **Averages and isolated query speed can hide tail or workload-wide regressions.**', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

for (const [groupId, items] of Object.entries(groupItems)) {
  const group = manifest.inventory.find(row => row.id === groupId);
  const section = items.map(bullet).join('\n\n');
  const comparison = group.items.length > 1 ? 'These items answer different points in the evidence chain. Live DMVs describe transient requests/tasks; cumulative DMVs describe a counter window; Query Store persists interval history; Extended Events capture selected event timelines; OS counters add shared-system context. Within each family, correlate scope and lifetime before combining values. A plan forcing or tuning recommendation is a mitigation decision after comparison, not raw diagnostic evidence.' : 'No within-group comparison applies.';
  const code = groupId === 'diagnostic-signals-and-tools' ? '```sql\n-- Snapshot completed waits for a known interval; this does not show current waits.\nSELECT wait_type, waiting_tasks_count, wait_time_ms\nFROM sys.dm_os_wait_stats\nORDER BY wait_time_ms DESC;\n\n-- Current waits require a separate live snapshot.\nSELECT session_id, wait_type, wait_duration_ms, blocking_session_id\nFROM sys.dm_os_waiting_tasks;\n```' : groupId === 'bottleneck-analysis' ? '```text\nObservation: endpoint p95 rose from 180 ms to 900 ms; request rate is stable.\nEvidence: SQL requests show PAGEIOLATCH waits and a 3x increase in logical reads.\nHypothesis: the endpoint now processes more pages; storage latency alone is not yet established.\nNext: compare query plan/parameter cohort and file-level I/O deltas for the same interval.\n```' : '```text\nHypothesis: candidate covering index reduces reads for one measured endpoint.\nControl: replay the same parameter distribution and concurrent write mix.\nGuardrails: p99, successful throughput, CPU/operation, logical reads/operation, write latency.\nDecision: retain only if user impact improves without violating workload guardrails.\n```';
  const name = 'reference-' + groupId;
  await write(name, [head + ' — Reference: ' + group.title, '', '# Scope and version', '', 'SQL Server 2025 (17.x) is the current scope. This catalog is bounded to the exact inventory names below. DMV permissions, counter scope, and feature eligibility vary by object/version; use the linked specific documentation for the target build.', '', '# How this group fits the mental model', '', groupId === 'diagnostic-signals-and-tools' ? 'Choose evidence based on the question: current activity, historical query behavior, event details, or host pressure. A trustworthy timeline connects these sources without pretending their counters have identical lifetime or attribution.' : groupId === 'bottleneck-analysis' ? 'A bottleneck is a constrained resource relative to a workload, not just a metric with a large number. Link the user symptom to query-level work, wait mechanism, and shared resource before selecting a remedy.' : 'Tuning is a controlled experiment. The reference items describe a measurement discipline and common interventions; none guarantees success without representative validation.', '', '# Supplied names explained', '', section, '', '# Cross-item comparison', '', comparison, '', '# Examples', '', code, '', 'The code is a diagnostic pattern, not a claim that it was executed against your server. Grant only the permissions needed for the target DMVs. For actual tuning, record the workload, build, interval, and output alongside the result.', '', '# Common mistakes', '', '- Reading cumulative counters as if they represented the incident window.', '- Assigning root cause from one wait type, missing-index suggestion, or average.', '- Comparing plan or file counters from different reset periods.', '- Collecting broad event streams indefinitely, or treating a bounded ring buffer as permanent history.', '- Forcing a plan or changing parallelism without a verification and removal path.', '- Declaring a win from one isolated query while ignoring p95/p99, throughput, writes, and other query regressions.', '', '# Knowledge checks with answers', '', groupId === 'diagnostic-signals-and-tools' ? '1. Which DMV provides current waiting tasks? **`sys.dm_os_waiting_tasks`; `sys.dm_os_wait_stats` is completed cumulative history.**\n2. Which tool preserves plan/runtime history by interval? **Query Store, when enabled and capturing.**\n3. Is a ring buffer durable? **No; it is in-memory and bounded.**\n4. Is a missing-index row a design command? **No; it is a limited candidate to validate.**' : groupId === 'bottleneck-analysis' ? '1. Does `PAGEIOLATCH` distinguish slow storage from excessive reads? **No; both evidence classes matter.**\n2. What does `RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE` signify? **A query is waiting for an execution memory grant.**\n3. Is `CXCONSUMER` always actionable as a defect? **No; interpret parallel coordination with plan behavior and skew.**\n4. Are application pool exhaustion and `THREADPOOL` the same? **No; one is in the client pool, the other is SQL Server worker availability.**' : '1. Why replay realistic concurrent writes for an index test? **Indexes add write and storage costs that a read-only test misses.**\n2. What complements p95 when checking a release? **p50/p99, errors, successful throughput, and normalized CPU/reads.**\n3. Why define rollback triggers first? **They make the response to a harmful canary measurable and quick.**\n4. Why change one factor? **To attribute observed outcomes to the intervention.**', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));
}

await write('practice', [head + ' — Exercises and worked solutions', '', '# Goals', '', 'Practice turning a symptom into evidence, distinguishing queues, and choosing measurable, reversible experiments.', '', '# Worked example', '', 'A nightly report’s p95 rises after a statistics refresh. Query Store shows a new plan and more CPU, but interval averages combine parameter values. First capture the report’s parameter distribution and the plan/runtime rows for a comparable window. Verify whether the new plan is responsible for the affected cohort; inspect CPU, reads, waits, and concurrency. Test a bounded mitigation with both typical and extreme parameters. Compare p50/p95/p99, throughput, errors, CPU and reads per execution. If a forced plan is used as a short-term mitigation, verify successful forcing and define when/how to unforce it.', '', '# Exercises', '', 'Try each scenario before reading its solution.', '', '1. Instance wait counters show many hours of PAGEIOLATCH waits, but the issue started ten minutes ago. What evidence do you collect next?', '2. `WRITELOG` rises during a write-heavy workload. List two distinct hypotheses and the measurements that separate them.', '3. An app reports timeout, but Query Store does not show a slow query. Trace the likely boundary sequence.', '4. A candidate index makes one SELECT twice as fast but increases p99 write latency and storage. Decide what to do and what extra evidence is needed.', '5. After upgrading to SQL Server 2025, a team attributes an improvement to OPPO. What eligibility and evidence must be checked?', '6. A plan forced through Query Store stops being used. What does this mean, and what should operations verify?', '', '# Progressive hints', '', '1. Compare a before/after interval and use live waits for “now”; check reset/start time and query attribution.', '2. Separate flush latency from commit frequency/transaction shape.', '3. An application timer can include pool acquisition and client/network work outside SQL execution.', '4. The affected SELECT is one part of the workload; quantify total read/write mix and user SLOs.', '5. SQL Server 2025 does not imply every feature is active; check compatibility level 170 for OPPO and observe eligible query behavior.', '6. Plan forcing is an attempt to reuse an eligible recorded plan, not a guarantee.', '', '# Complete solutions', '', '1. Timestamp a fresh snapshot; inspect `sys.dm_os_waiting_tasks`/active requests and the affected query’s Query Store history. Compare wait deltas over the incident window, file-I/O latency and volume, plan/parameter mix, and deployment changes. Historical cumulative totals cannot prove the ten-minute cause.', '2. Hypothesis A: log device flush latency. Check log-file write latency/stalls and storage path contention. Hypothesis B: excessive synchronous commits or tiny transaction batches. Check commit rate, transaction size, and application call pattern. Both can coexist; compare latency per flush and work per operation.', '3. Follow application request start, pool-acquisition duration, connection checkout, SQL request begin/end, network transfer, timeout/cancel, and retry. Check whether SQL work continues after the client deadline and whether connections are held/leaked. Query Store aggregation can also hide a short event or wrong query identity.', '4. Do not deploy based on the SELECT microbenchmark alone. Run a representative mixed read/write workload, quantify storage and write latency changes, other plans, concurrency, tail latency, and throughput. Keep the index only if aggregate user outcomes and capacity goals justify the cost; otherwise revise or reject it.', '5. OPPO is supported in SQL Server 2025 at compatibility level 170. Check database compatibility, feature/database-scoped configuration and query eligibility, plan variants/Query Store evidence, parameter distribution, and a controlled before/after workload. Do not attribute a generic version-upgrade improvement to OPPO without evidence. [IQP feature requirements](' + urls.iq + ')', '6. Inspect Query Store and the plan-forcing failure event, compatibility and query context, and whether the recorded plan remains eligible. Compare live plan and performance; use a tested alternate mitigation or unforce, then verify the regression under representative parameters. [Plan forcing behavior](' + urls.qsTune + ')', '', '# Tests and edge cases', '', '- Compare at least one steady window and one expected peak window.', '- Include parameter extremes and relevant concurrency, data volume, and background jobs.', '- Verify DMV permissions under a least-privilege diagnostic principal.', '- Label counter snapshots with engine start/reset, database, and timestamps.', '- Test cancellation/retry behavior and connection disposal after timeouts.', '- Set canary rollback triggers for errors, p99, throughput, CPU, log latency, and storage headroom.', '- Check that Query Store remains in its intended capture state and has sufficient storage/retention.', '', '# Reflection questions', '', 'Which part of the user-visible timer is outside SQL execution? Which metric identifies scope versus cause? What measurement would disprove your hypothesis? What other workload can pay the cost of your fix? Who can reverse it, and what exact signal triggers rollback?', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

await write('interview', [head + ' — Interview questions and reasoning', '', '# Core questions', '', '1. How do you start diagnosing “SQL Server is slow”?', '2. What is the difference between `sys.dm_os_wait_stats` and `sys.dm_os_waiting_tasks`?', '3. Why use Query Store if the plan cache has plans?', '4. How should a team interpret a dominant wait type?', '5. How do you distinguish database time from connection-pool delay?', '6. How do you safely evaluate an index or query hint?', '7. What are the limits of a missing-index recommendation?', '8. What should be measured before and after a performance change?', '9. What does SQL Server 2025 change for Query Store on readable secondaries and OPPO?', '10. What is a safe operational use of Query Store plan forcing?', '', '# Concise interview answers', '', '1. Bound the symptom by workload, time, user impact, and changes; compare baseline, current requests, query history, and correlated resources.', '2. Wait stats are completed cumulative waits since reset/start; waiting tasks are a current snapshot and neither alone attributes the whole workload.', '3. Query Store persists query/plan and interval runtime history after plans leave cache and supports regression comparisons, subject to capture/storage configuration.', '4. Treat it as a hypothesis generator; correlate query, time window, plan, resource counters, and competing causes.', '5. Measure app pool acquisition and request stages separately from SQL request execution and return.', '6. Specify a predicted metric and representative workload, change one factor, canary, monitor broader costs, and document reversal triggers.', '7. It is an incomplete optimizer signal that omits important design and workload costs; merge/validate candidates with full workload evidence.', '8. Latency distribution, successful throughput, errors, CPU and reads per operation, writes/log latency, storage/capacity, and workload context.', '9. Secondary Query Store is available starting SQL Server 2025 but opt-in; OPPO is available in SQL Server 2025 at compatibility level 170.', '10. Use a recorded plan as a measured temporary mitigation, check forcing outcome, monitor its fit, and keep an unforce/rollback path.', '', '# Deep follow-up answers', '', 'Explain counter lifetimes and permissions. For waits, distinguish PAGEIOLATCH from in-memory PAGELATCH and avoid assigning cause from labels. Query Store runtime rows aggregate by interval and plan, which may mix parameter values; query text/context and distributions matter. Query Store forcing can fail. A successful forced plan may still become unsuitable as data distribution or workload changes. An index can reduce reads yet increase write cost, storage, and maintenance. SQL Server 2022+ DMV permissions differ from older guidance, so diagnostic access should be granted specifically.', '', '# Scenario questions', '', '**A dashboard shows CPU low, yet an API is slow.** Inspect connection acquisition, current waits/blockers, network/serialization, external dependencies, and timeouts; low instance CPU does not rule out queueing elsewhere.', '', '**A query regression follows an engine upgrade.** Compare Query Store plan history and compatibility level, workload and parameter distribution, waits, configuration, and build-specific changes. Establish the causal chain before rolling back a version or adding a hint.', '', '**Most server waits are parallelism waits.** Identify affected queries and plans, worker skew, runnable CPU demand, duration, and throughput. Do not globally change MAXDOP or cost threshold because a wait label dominates.', '', '# Tradeoffs and alternatives', '', 'DMVs offer low-latency point-in-time or cumulative evidence but are volatile and permission-sensitive. Query Store preserves database-local history with storage and capture costs. Extended Events offer precise event evidence with collection/retention costs. OS counters reveal shared host pressure but not query identity. Plan forcing and hints can restore stability quickly but age and need active maintenance.', '', '# Common weak answers', '', '- “The top wait is the root cause.”', '- “High logical reads means the disk is slow.”', '- “Query Store is always enabled on every database.”', '- “A missing-index recommendation should be created as written.”', '- “The query got faster, so production got faster.”', '- “A timeout proves SQL Server canceled the request.”', '- “Set MAXDOP to one to remove parallel waits.”', '', '# Self-check rubric', '', 'Score each answer 0–2: 0 = names a metric/fix only; 1 = explains the mechanism but misses scope or alternatives; 2 = states evidence lifetime and attribution, proposes a falsifiable test, includes workload-wide tradeoffs, and gives a rollback criterion.', '', '# Sources', '', bibliography].join('\n'));

console.log('Wrote t03-013 drafts for ' + allItems.length + ' exact inventory entries across ' + (Object.keys(groupItems).length + 4) + ' lessons.');
